+# Atomic tight-surface experiment
+
+Unpublished performance prerequisite for the planned frontal world-Z API. The flight API draft remains frozen separately. This experiment is not enabled by package registration or production entrypoints.
+
+## Render and presentation contracts
+
+`TightSurfaceRenderer` records one frame's draw passes, measures referenced geometry, and allocates a grow-only16px-aligned crop inside the unchanged virtual framebuffer. WebGL keeps the original projection and full virtual viewport, offset by the integer crop. Canvas retains the original full-frame vertex coordinates and global pixel sample positions; only pixel/depth storage, clearing and image copying use the crop. Existing DPR, backing-size limits, antialiasing, MSAA and runtime cadence are not reduced.
+
+A private OffscreenCanvas is never implicitly displayed. The source transfers one ImageBitmap with generation, frame ID, command revision, rendered snapshot, crop and applied CSS dimensions. Only one transferred frame is outstanding. While main is busy, rendering may continue but no additional bitmap is created; the next matching acknowledgement transfers the latest complete frame. Paused/static rendering does not repeat transfers.
+
+Main owns every received bitmap until `transferFromImageBitmap` consumes it or `close()` drops it. Stale generations, duplicate/out-of-order frames, replacement, invalid metadata and teardown are handled explicitly. Crop CSS, backing dimensions and matching bitmap are committed in one RAF callback. Observable frame snapshots advance only after that transaction. Complete events are held until their rendered frame is presented.
+
+Metadata is latched at actual rendered-frame boundaries. Applied CSS dimensions are captured in the renderer adapter's resize call, not when a command arrives. Hidden resize commands cannot relabel an older bitmap. Synchronous rendering for asynchronous PNG encoding is latched before subsequent commands can change state.
+
+`transferToImageBitmap` clears its source. Fresh capture therefore forces a render and reads it before transfer. The adapter exposes virtual full dimensions and expands crop bytes without resampling, preserving full-frame bottom-up premultiplied captures and PNG exports. Synchronous worker capture reconstructs the last presented bitmap; synchronous local capture can render fresh pixels.
+
+## Current validation and limitations
+
+Focused tests cover exact expanded Canvas pixels across shapes, accessories/fades, size160/320/640/1024, DPR1/2/3, aspect ratios, zoom, queued draw failures, ownership/drop/disposal, hidden resize metadata and async transfer failure. Actual browser worker/main checks are a separate gate.
+
+The first independent Node ABBA measurement did **not** show a CPU improvement: tight rendering was roughly equal or slightly slower. Reduced backing area is not evidence of reduced CPU usage. Matched real-browser/process CPU trials including bitmap transfer and presentation remain required. GPU savings are unverified and are not claimed.
+
+Known acceptance gaps include crop-aware full interaction boxes, entrance/occluder clipping, full recovery/visibility regression coverage, and the previous GPU experiment's non-identical edge pixels in some cases. Do not publish this experiment or claim the canvas-performance problem is solved until these are resolved and CPU improvement is independently demonstrated.
+
+## Run
+
+    node experiments/atomic-surface/build.mjs
+    node --import tsx --test tests/atomic-presenter.test.ts tests/atomic-producer.test.ts tests/atomic-bridge.test.ts tests/render-surface.test.ts tests/canvas-tight-surface.test.ts
+    npx tsc -p experiments/atomic-surface/tsconfig.json
+
+Serve dist and open `/atomic-surface/index.html`. The visible harness compares baseline, atomic worker and atomic main fallback. It is a correctness harness, not a single-instance CPU benchmark. The independent benchmark uses matched single-instance windows and reports renderer, process CPU and presentation costs separately.
